<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Renaissance</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Renaissance was a period of cultural revival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from the fourteenth to the sixteenth century, beginning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and centred on the recovery of classical learning. It drew on Greek texts and scholars from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, on Arabic science from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> and on the legacy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. The term is also applied to earlier revivals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-renaissance">Carolingian Renaissance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/renaissance.jpg" alt="Renaissance" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sandro Botticelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sandro_Botticelli_046.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Many scholars place the origin of the Renaissance in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> at the turn of the 13th and 14th centuries, with the writings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and the paintings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto di Bondone</a>. One proposed starting point is 1401, when Lorenzo Ghiberti and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a> competed for the contract for the bronze doors of the Baptistery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>. Italy was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> and was one of the most urbanised areas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, with many cities standing among Roman ruins.</p><p>Scholars have pointed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 to 1350 and to the patronage of the Medici banking family, but no single factor fully explains why it began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>. Historians disagree on whether the Renaissance broke with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> or extended them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Renaissance humanism was less a philosophy than a method of learning. Humanists studied ancient texts in their original languages and appraised them with reasoning and empirical evidence, rather than resolving contradictions between authors as medieval scholastics did. Their education rested on the Studia Humanitatis: poetry, grammar, history, moral philosophy and rhetoric. In the 14th century scholars such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poggio-bracciolini">Poggio Bracciolini</a> searched European libraries for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works by authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretius">Lucretius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>. By the early 15th century a Greek phase was under way, recovering Greek literary, historical and theological texts.</p><p>In art, the Renaissance developed linear perspective. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a> is credited with first treating a painting as a window into space, and Brunelleschi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a> formalised the technique. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">Printing</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>, from a single shop in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> around 1440, had spread to around 270 cities and produced more than 20 million volumes by the end of the 15th century. Navigation also advanced: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> sailed in 1492 and the Magellan–Elcano expedition made the first circumnavigation between 1519 and 1522.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> the Renaissance spread across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and by the 16th century through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, changing as it met local cultures. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press marked its arrival in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> was the best-known humanist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, and the Elizabethan era is usually seen as the height of the English Renaissance. Many leading theologians, including Erasmus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huldrych-zwingli">Huldrych Zwingli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-more">Thomas More</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-calvin">John Calvin</a>, followed the humanist method.</p><p>The artist and writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a> first used the term rinascita in The Lives of the Artists (1550). The word Renaissance comes from French and was popularised by the historian Jules Michelet in 1855. The term has also been applied to earlier revivals, such as the Carolingian and Ottonian Renaissances and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance-of-the-12th-century">Renaissance of the 12th century</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Renaissance” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The building programme begun under Pericles, including the Parthenon (447–432 BCE) designed by Ictinus and Callicrates with sculpture overseen by Phidias, and the Propylaea and Erechtheion, set a standard for architecture copied in Rome and again from the <b>Renaissance</b> onward.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Italian legal scholars transmitted it to medieval and <b>Renaissance</b> Europe, where it became the foundation of the civil law tradition that shapes the legal systems of much of the world today.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…Orientalist policy gave way to Anglicisation: the Asiatick Society of William Jones (1784), the Benares Sanskrit College (1791) and the College of Fort William (1800), which helped the Bengal <b>Renaissance</b>, were followed by Thomas Babington Macaulay's Minute on Indian Education of 1835 and the Education Dispatch of 1854.</blockquote><a href="/empire/company-rule-in-india">Company rule in India · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Duchy of Milan was one of the great centres of the <b>Italian Renaissance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Medici Tuscany remained one of the great centres of European culture after the <b>Renaissance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Grand Duchy of Tuscany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bibliotheca Corviniana of Matthias Corvinus was one of the largest libraries of <b>Renaissance</b> Europe; over two hundred of its volumes survive in collections across the world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
